Food?  C'mon!  Every tourist enters the dungeon with 10-20 food items,
and they don't need to follow any special diet.  Especially combined
with prayer, those should well last until Sokoban.  The HP are a real
issue though – I usually drink one of the potions of extra healing right
at the start (blessed if possible) to start with 15 instead of 10 HP.  I
keep the other one for close calls.

(If food management is hard for you, you should try the convict patch by
Karl Garrison.  After starving several times in a row, you do learn
something about that topic…)  ;-)

>> -- an early shop with speed boots or something,
>> and you have an edge. They aren't unusually weak
>> or stupid, or have any odd restrictions.
> Any role can just happen upon helpful
> stuff in the early game, but that's not
> reliable.
The first mines level is more reliable in that way.  Especially if you
stay on the upstairs and kill some dwarfs there.  My early gear often
comes exclusively from dwarfs:

- 6 Dwarvish mithril coat (MC3)
- 0 Dwarvish cloak (MC2)
- 2 Dwarvish iron helm
- 2 Dwarvish round shield
- 2 Iron shoes

That's AC -2 in total (or AC4 when there is no mithril, but then usually
some other suit can reduce AC to around 0) at dungeon level 3-5.  MC is
3 (2 without mithril).  I use my pet for curse-testing, so it might be 1
or 2 more points for positive enchantments (early enchantment scrolls go
to the shirt, which lasts until the end).

> One problem for Tourists is running out
> of projectiles in the early game.  If your
> initial stack of darts is not particularly large
> and also not blessed, this can be a big
> problem, unless you get lucky with early
> daggers.
Same source – the mines provide plenty of daggers, and just lingering
around the upstairs can give you several.  With only a few daggers, I
throw those first and the darts afterwards to reduce the loss (and train
the dagger skill).

As a rule, I also disarm early dart traps, which can yield a large
number of darts.  There is a chance that a poisonous dart can kill you
in the process, though.  That has not yet happened to me, but is
possible through this chain of events:

1. trap does not get disarmed (?)
2. trap still has darts (14/15)
3. dart is poisoned (1/6)
4. dart hits you (?)
5. poison is deadly (1/10)

1 should have something to do with one of your attributes (probably
dexterity), and 4 mainly with your AC.  Without considering those, the
chance is below 1.6% per attempt.

